<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsonga people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tsonga are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, with smaller communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a>; their language is Xitsonga. Their chiefdoms around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a>, among them the Tembe, traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and other goods with Portuguese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English and Austrian merchants from the 16th century. In the 19th century many came under the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsonga-people.jpg" alt="Tsonga people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Washiucho · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Gazankulu.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
